<div class="meta">Lesson 0017 · Rendering · from issue #65 (road tiles)</div>
<h1>Tiling Modular Kit Pieces over a Parametric Grid</h1>
<p>Modular asset kits (KayKit, Synty, Kenney) ship roads, walls, and floors
as <em>tiles</em>: origin-centered, uniform footprint, designed to butt
seamlessly. The skill is not placing them by hand — it's deriving every
placement from the same constants that define your world.</p>

<h2>Anchor the tile grid to the world's landmarks</h2>
<p>The downtown layout is parametric: 3×3 blocks on a 64-unit pitch, 16-unit
streets centered at ±32, map edge at ±110. The naive tiling — start at one
edge and step by tile size — misaligns everything that matters: 220/16 is
not an integer, and junction art must land <em>exactly</em> where streets
cross.</p>
<p>Anchor to the landmarks instead: junctions at (±32,±32) are multiples of
16, so putting tile <em>centers</em> at multiples of 16 makes every junction
a tile center by construction. Then:</p>
<pre><code>t = -96, -80, ..., 96          13 tiles, covering ±104
t == ±32   → junction tile     (both streets pass through; draw once)
|‖t|-32| == 16 → zebra tile    (the arm beside each junction)
±(104..110)  → end cap         (a compressed tile fills the last 6 units)</code></pre>
<p>The leftover 6 units at each edge get a tile <em>fitted</em> to the odd
footprint — the Fill contract handles non-square targets, and squished lane
dashes at the map's far edge are invisible in practice.</p>

<h2>Scale footprint and thickness separately</h2>
<p>The tiles are 2×2×0.1. Filling a 16-unit street means scaling XZ by 8 —
but scaling Y by 8 too would raise a 0.8-unit curb over what collision
treats as flat ground. Players would see a knee-high sidewalk they can walk
through. The fix: XZ scale comes from the model bounds (Fill contract),
Y is pinned to a constant 0.05 sliver.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>General rule.</strong> Ground tiles scale
in-plane only. Any vertical feature (curb, rail) modeled into a ground tile
becomes a collision lie the moment you scale it.</div>

<h2>Rotation happens in model space</h2>
<p><code>DrawModelEx</code> applies <em>scale, then rotation, then
translation</em>. A 90°-yawed tile therefore has its model-space X axis
covering the world's Z span — the scale computation must swap axes when the
rotation flag is set. Getting this wrong on non-square fits stretches the
lane dashes sideways.</p>

<h2>Always keep the primitive fallback</h2>
<p>The old asphalt strips didn't die: they render whenever the road models
are missing, preserving the "clone the repo without running
<code>fetch_assets.sh</code>" path. A renderer that requires optional assets
is a renderer with a mandatory dependency.</p>

<div class="callout good"><strong>Cost check.</strong> 4 street runs × 13
tiles + 8 end caps ≈ 60 <code>DrawModelEx</code> calls of a 2-triangle-dozen
mesh — noise next to the skinned characters. Tiling is cheap; don't
prematurely batch.</div>
